-The same paper offers a useful negative result. Across the tested sucrose concentrations, the abstract reported no significant change in the binding constant or binding-site number; the detailed results also showed no significant sugar-concentration effect on the binding constants for lactose or sucrose.[7] Lactose did affect the estimated number of accessible sites.[7] Thus, replacing one sugar system with another is not adequately described as changing sweetness while leaving the pigment environment untouched—but adding sugar is not automatically an effective binding treatment either.
+The same paper offers a useful negative result. At 35°C across the tested 0–150 mM added-sugar range, Figure 4 shows no significant concentration effect on the binding constant for either lactose or sucrose, or on the fitted binding-site number for sucrose.[7] Some lactose concentrations did differ in the estimated number of accessible sites.[7] Thus, replacing one sugar system with another is not adequately described as changing sweetness while leaving the pigment environment untouched—but adding sugar is not automatically an effective binding treatment either.
 
